A||N||21||Rumor||Should I install an rear antisway bar?||||1||
Z||000000||Rumor||11-28-2001||01:05 AM||||Will it dramatically improve handling?||||reg||1
Z||000001||clayatandalusia||11-28-2001||01:08 AM||||good question, i've been thinking about doing that but i thought id rebuild the a-frames int he front and rear thought it would help more||||reg||1
Z||000002||rodmcneill||11-28-2001||01:17 AM||||Yes you should, one of the best "bang for the bucks" you can do on a Fiero. It keeps the rear end a lot flatter going around curves.||||reg||14
Z||000003||ray b||11-28-2001||01:31 AM||||HOW GOOD A DRIVER ARE YOU????<BR>it will make the car  more neutral<BR>more balanced but with out as much understeer and will go in to oversteer <BR>eazyer.<BR> will your style of driving benefit or will oversteer bite you????<P>one way to find out is SIM/game GPL<BR>models 67 mid-eng gp cars before wings and slicks and a good model of over/understeer transitions FREE DEMO AT  <A HREF="http://www.papy.com/gpl/gpl.download.index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.papy.com/gpl/gpl.download.index.html</A> <BR>get the 1.2 patch and the speed upgrade if you need it to.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||California Kid||11-28-2001||09:41 AM||||Caution note: When installing the rear sway bar, be very careful drilling the lower control arms. When the drill brakes through it can pull inself right on up into your CV Joint Boot (which are a pain in the butt to replace).<P>The car just loves the rear sway bar, you can't go wrong.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/californiakidsig_2.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000005||mshill||11-28-2001||01:28 PM||||Probably the most cost effective suspension upgrade for the Fiero.  But make sure you take your time to get to know the handling of your "new" car.  It will handle significantly different than before.||||reg||1
Z||000006||kevin||11-28-2001||01:46 PM||||I was thinking of doing the same thing.I heard that you can put a Fiero front sway bar on the rear. SImply find a used one from a friend or recycler and turn it around so the bar faces in the oppisite direction from the front one. I never did this myself. I want to do it soon. Anyone out there accomplished this upgrade? Keep this going.||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||11-28-2001||01:52 PM||||You will love the feel of the rear sway bar.<BR>In corners it is awsome, but like everyone says get to know the handling first.  Very easy to do donuts with it installed.  My friend and I did mine for under $20.  Not hard at all to do, just take your time.<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forum_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000008||The Aura||11-28-2001||01:58 PM||||I have heard about doing the front sawaybar on the rear aswell and know it would be a lot cheaper that buying a rear bar from a place like the fiero store. I have heard it can be mounted backwards or in a standard direction. Who out there has a stock front bar on the rear... how did you go about putting it in.<P>------------------<BR>1986 GT 4spd&gt; Suspension Techniques Springs, Poly Bushings, Bosal Brospeed 4.5" Exhaust Tips, Cross Drilled Rotors||||reg||1
Z||000009||VISCERAL||11-28-2001||02:22 PM||||On the yahoo fiero racer forum one guy takes late model camaro/TA sway bars which is hollow and 1.25" and takes an oxy-acetylin torch to them on the bends and reshapes them to match the fiero's swaybar profile. They're bigger, lighter and stiffer than stock. All around winner. Another guy mounts them not under the rear cradle but over them, with collars and heim joints. You gain more ground clearance than the standard method. I believe they(camaro/ta) might also have a 1" bar that we can adapt. Will do research unless someone in a warmer climate could do it, it will take me a while.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||11-30-2001||09:05 PM||||The heim joints,or rod ends will help out the ride,and help to eliminate the sudden whip-factor.The standard,outer bushings are easier to use/install most of the time,but can bind up the movement back there as well;they're flat,not conducive to movement.||||reg||1
Z||000011||MrPBody||11-30-2001||09:16 PM||||My '85 SE has a Fiero Store 1" front bar and a stock 7/8" front bar mounted on the rear.  I am very happy with the combination, and the rear bar was fairly easy to install.<P>Here are <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/weekend/swaybar/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Randy Agee's instructions</A>.  I did it more-or-less this way, although my links are a little different.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||ToddAsh||11-30-2001||10:23 PM|||| I installed the front on the rear as the above instructions.Handles great but I keep breaking the long link bolts on one side or the other.Gave up and took it off a few weeks ago.Do I need different bolts or a lighter foot on the curves?||||reg||1
Z||000013||MrPBody||12-01-2001||02:22 PM||||ToddAsh --<P>Try Grade 8 bolts, if you're not using them already, although Grade 5 bolts should be strong enough if they are under tension load only.<P>A couple of possible problems:<P>The link-bolt holes you drilled in the control arms may be too small, and are not allowing the link bolts to pivot freely.  These holes should be about 5/8" diameter to fit the bushing shoulder.<P>The link-bolt holes may be too far apart or too close together, laterally.  This could place bending loads on the link bolts as the suspension moves.<P>Good luck fixing this.  So far, mine is working OK.  &lt;crosses fingers&gt;<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||12-01-2001||04:34 PM||||When drilling the holes in the A arm..... Slide some plywood or metal bewteen the arm and CV to protect the boot when the drill punches thru.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Rumor||12-01-2001||07:09 PM||||That's exactly what I did today when I put the bar in - I put a bar of steel there.  Thanks for all your input!<P>-Chris||||reg||1
Z||000016||shark93726||12-02-2001||06:31 AM||||A rear anti sway bar added to a Fiero, with no other suspension changes, (stock alignment, etc.) will dramatically CHANGE handling, but not necesarily IMPROVE handling.<P>You will go from a car with consistant push in the front suspension to one with dramatic looseness of the rear end, which, while it will FEEL better in corners because the front will turn in better without pushing, the car will probably have little or no improvement in autocross times, and may actually end up slower, because you will be less able to power throttle out of corners onto straights, which is the most important thing to improve times.<P>If you change the front bar to a stiffer one when you add a rear bar, then the car can end up with a little better handling, and be fairly neutral. <P>But I feel that additional camber and caster, with solid front sway bar mounts and solid front suspension bushings, will end up with a much better handling and quicker car. Of course, that sacrifices ride a little, and takes a lot more work and money.<P>Speed costs money, how fast did you want to go?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com)||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fierowrecker||12-02-2001||12:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rumor:<BR><B>Will it dramatically improve handling?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Rumor!<BR>I have a front bar added to the rear of mine, and I would not remove it!!!<BR>It is a good improvement to the suspention and well worth the time to add it...<BR>I am able to take corners much faster, without the front end plowing out of the corner...<P><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||filthyscarecrow||12-02-2001||07:46 PM||||i don't get it. why are we drilling the lower a-arms? there are already holes in them for the anti-roll bars. i put a front bar on the back of my 85GT. had to drill 4 holes- 2 for each bushing bracket on the bottom of the cradle. bar costs $25 from local "recycler." <P>i would also upgrade all the bushings to poly if you're gonna do it, otherwise why bother. poly antiroll bar bushings are cheap too. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ed||12-02-2001||10:16 PM||||My experience with the holes in the A-arms also.  No drilling needed there, only in the frame members. <P>Tie a little piece of thread to any bolts or nuts you may lose in a channel or box member.  Break off the thread when installed.  Same for installing trailer hitches.<P>I also recommend poly for the sway bar attachments at all points.  <P>I've had (Whitney) anti-sway or roll bars on my two (4 cyl. and 6 cyl.) Fieros since 1984.  Highly recommended.  I've never noticed a tendency to oversteer, even with power slides on the track.||||reg||1
Z||000020||MrPBody||12-03-2001||09:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>i don't get it. why are we drilling the lower a-arms?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Although my '85 SE does have existing holes in the rear control arms, they don't align with the anti-roll bar eyes.<P><BR>Also . . .<P>Shark93726 has a point.  A rear bar will help, but it should be used in combination with other modifications, particularly a larger front bar.  On my car, mods include:<P>&gt; mild lowering<P>&gt; adjustable upper ball joints<P>&gt; a tad of negative camber on all wheels<P>&gt; Grand Am front wheels<P>&gt; no steering damper<P>&gt; slightly narrower front tires<P>&gt; front bar on rear<P>&gt; 1" front bar<P><BR>It's a never-ending quest.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||thomas_l||12-03-2001||10:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by shark93726:<BR><B>the car will probably have little or no improvement in autocross times, and may actually end up slower, because you will be less able to power throttle out of corners onto straights<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is why a rear bar is at the bottom of my priority list. With 168k on the odo there are plenty of other needy areas to work on that will net better autocross performance  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||6
